 ID.<br>
<br>
In light of the ongoing discussion for the DTLS 1.2 connection ID, I<br>
just want to walk through a few points here and confirm that we are in<br>
good shape.<br>
<br>
- (D)TLS 1.3 always requires AEAD ciphers<br>
- AEAD ciphers implicitly authenticate the AAD length<br>
- the connection ID is the only implicitly variable-length field in the<br>
=C2=A0 record header/AAD (the size of the sequence number and length fields=
<br>
=C2=A0 is indicated by bits in the first byte)<br>
- in light of the above three points, the length of the connection ID is<br=
>
=C2=A0 also implicitly authenticated by the AEAD<br>
- the AAD explicitly flags whether or not a CID is present at all<br>
- the AEAD implicitly authenticates the length of the ciphertext, so it<br>
=C2=A0 is okay to omit the length from the AAD when the L bit is 0.<br>
<br>
Section 4.1<br>
<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0-=C2=A0 If the first byte is alert(21), handshake(22), or ack(=
proposed,<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 26), the record MUST be interpreted as a DTLSPlaintext=
 record.<br>
<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0-=C2=A0 If the first byte is any other value, then receivers M=
UST check to<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 see if the leading bits of the first byte are 001.=C2=
=A0 If so, the<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 implementation MUST process the record as DTLSCipherte=
xt; the true<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 content type will be inside the protected portion.<br>
<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0-=C2=A0 Otherwise, the record MUST be rejected as if it had fa=
iled<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 deprotection, as described in Section 4.5.2.<br>
<br>
I can imagine a reading of this last point that says that something that<br=
>
implements DTLS 1.3 MUST treat a tls12_cid ContentType as an error ...<br>
even if that software also supports DTLS 1.2.=C2=A0 I could imagine a<br>
phrasing like &quot;not a valid DTLS 1.3 record&quot; that would not have t=
his<br>
property, but I am not sure whether that is the right approach.<br>
In particular, when CIDs are in use, we do not necessarily have any<br>
external context to indicate whether we should be expecting DTLS 1.3 or<br>
DTLS 1.2 (or either) on a given listening socket.<br>
(I guess a mixed 1.2+1.3 implementation would also get Application Data,<br=
>
but 5-tuple would in theory be a distinguisher for when to accept that.)<br=
>
<br>
Section 4.2.3<br>
<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0In DTLS 1.3, when records are encrypted, record sequence numbe=
rs are<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0also encrypted.=C2=A0 The basic pattern is that the underlying=
 encryption<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0algorithm used with the AEAD algorithm is used to generate a m=
ask<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0which is then XORed with the sequence number.<br>
<br>
I have mixed feelings about breaking through the AEAD abstraction to<br>
assume that there is an &quot;underlying encryption algorithm&quot; to use.=
<br>
Furthermore, we currently only cover AES and ChaCha20, though the<br>
ciphersuites registry lists TLS 1.3 ciphers with SM4 as block cipher, as<br=
>
well as MAC-only ciphersuites.=C2=A0 (Presumably the lack of sequence numbe=
r<br>
encryption is considered a feature for MAC-only ciphersuites.)=C2=A0 We may=
<br>
even want to limit our specification of sequence number encryption to<br>
specific named ciphersuite codepoints, instead of having the vague<br>
attempt at applying to future AES-using or ChaCha20-using ciphers.<br>
<br>
It seems that we are in effect imposing a new requirement for a TLS 1.3<br>
cipher to get listed with DTLS-OK=3DY, namely, that it has a mechanism for<=
br>
generating a mask for encrypting sequence numbers.=C2=A0 I think we should<=
br>
make this new requirement more explicit, e.g., with an update to the<br>
IANA registry.=C2=A0 It would also be useful if we could give guidance to<b=
r>
future ciphersuite authors for how one could do this in general, though<br>
that would probably not be normative guidance.<br>
<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0When the AEAD is based on ChaCha20, then the mask is generated=
 by<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0treating the first 4 bytes of the ciphertext as the block coun=
ter and<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0the next 12 bytes as the nonce, passing them to the ChaCha20 b=
lock<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0function (Section 2.3 of [CHACHA]):<br>
<br>
I note that this is effectively random nonce selection since we treat<br>
the encryption function as a PRF.=C2=A0 The sn_key is only regenerated when=
 the<br>
traffic keys are refreshed, so we may have higher risk of nonce-reuse<br>
here than for the &quot;real&quot; packet encryption since the sequential n=
once<br>
values used for packet encryption have a lower collision probability<br>
(though the value of what would be exposed on nonce reuse for sequence<br>
number encryption is lower).=C2=A0 That said, we are only using the cipher<=
br>
portion and do not have an AEAD tag, to the really nasty consequences of<br=
>
nonce reuse are not in scope.=C2=A0 Nonetheless, perhaps updating the<br>
guidance on how often to rekey is in order (or at least running the<br>
numbers).<br>
<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0The encrypted sequence number is computed by XORing the leadin=
g bytes<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0of the Mask with the sequence number.=C2=A0 Decryption is acco=
mplished by<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0the same process.<br>
<br>
(This is fine.=C2=A0 There is maybe some aesthetic complaint about &quot;so=
metimes<br>
bit 0 of the Mask aplies to bit 32 of the sequence number and sometimes<br>
it applies to bit 40 of the sequence number&quot; but I am pretty sure it<b=
r>
doesn&#39;t matter.)<br>
