Section 5.7.3 has a discussion about the need for distinct state<br>
machines to be running concurrently, and interplay between the handshake<br=
>
layer and the record layer state machine that is needed in order to<br>
dispatch records across state machines.=C2=A0 Getting this right seems<br>
security relevant, so a reminder in this section might be in order.<br>
<br>
TLS 1.3 places fairly stringent requirements on non-replayability of<br>
0-RTT data; this would typically be done at the session ticket or<br>
ClientHello level.=C2=A0 DTLS has optional per-record replay protection.=C2=
=A0 As<br>
far as I can tell, the two are orthogonal; that is, there is no<br>
inherent need to use DTLS replay protection with 0-RTT data (but there<br>
is still need to prevent replay of ClientHellos, and thus the sets of<br>
data that follow them).=C2=A0 It may be worth reiterating that the two<br>
mechanisms play different roles, and the one cannot replace the other.<br>
<br>
If there is a large amount of skew between the send and receive epochs,<br>
the implementation will have to decide whether to keep around<br>
application traffic secrets or generate the various traffic keys and<br>
discart the oldest traffic secrets.<br>
